quotedTweet.text:
Well, personally, I think "Big Trucking" doesn't want a real driver shortage or else they'd have to pay their company drivers more... and treat them better to retain them...

Right now in trucking, there is a 90% or more turnover rate. Many drivers even jump ship within 90 days. They don't exit trucking outright. They just change trucking companies.

If the two biggest states could no longer churn out new CDL drivers, then what?  Who do you think doesn't want decertification of NY & CA? It's not the safety advocacy groups...

So, who else with significant influence would try to stop @SecDuffy from doing his duty under the law, which requires decertification of state CDL-issuing privileges whenever there is a final determination of a state's substantial non-compliance with 49 USC Chapter 313? Big Broker?

The fact is, those determinations have already been made against @nysdmv and @CA_DMV by @FMCSA but they were NOT decertified as the law requires. We
@theSBTC are suing over this in the US Court of Appeals for the DC Circuit in furtherance of highway safety.

We have asked @USDOT under FOIA for copies of requests made to the Office of the Secretary of Transportation & @FMCSA not to decertify. They failed to reply in a timely fashion (20 business days). The matter is currently under administrative appeal. The clock is ticking. They have another 20 days. Next, comes judicial review and scrutiny.

A subpoena duces tecum probably will just be issued in furtherance of our pending Petition for Review litigation in the U.S. Court of Appeals. If they still don't cooperate and provide the records, if any, there will be a motion to compel under penalty of contempt of court. Someone at USDOT or FMCSA could get locked up.

@TransportGOP @TransportDems @SenateCommerce and/or @CommerceDems could call Secretary Duffy and/or @BarrsDerek to testify about this at Congressional hearings.

Will they?

@RepDaveTaylor and the House Trucking Caucus should be looking into this... because something is very wrong here. Congressman Taylor already knows about an illegal immigrant trucker ring getting unlawfully licensed in NY and then exchanging their NY CDLs for OH CDLs... because we told his staff about it.

We suspect somebody very soon perhaps in the media is going to ask why such a zealous Administration stopped short at decertification... and whether they really had public safety in mind when deciding not to decertify these states from issuing CDLs as required by Federal law.

Safety advocates should be asking like Zach Cahalan @TruckSafetyOrg @SSAC9111 @SafeRoadsNow @Truck_Alliance but we hear crickets.

Maybe an investigative trucking news reporter, trucking personality, influencer, trucker advocate, or podcaster will chime in...

Do we still have any of those around?

@NoiMahoney @JohnHKingston @dr_phil_truckn @FreightWaves @ericdmiller46 @TransportTopics
@DanaGJournalist @TruckerTalk @ericharley @RedEyeRadioNet Mark Willis, KC Phillips @openroadsxm @RoadDogTrucking @TruckingDive
@alexjlockie @channel19todd @OverdriveUpdate

